---
// src/pages/chrome-extension-alternative.astro
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Chrome Bulk Image Downloader Alternative - Safe & Extension-Free"
  description="Looking for a Chrome bulk image downloader? Avoid browser extension security risks. Try this 100% private, client-side online alternative that requires no installation."
  keywords="chrome bulk image downloader, bulk image downloader chrome, chrome image downloader extension alternative"
>
  <!-- Hero Section -->
  <section
    class="relative overflow-hidden pt-12 pb-20 border-b border-hairline bg-canvas rounded-lg mb-12"
  >
    <div
      class="absolute inset-0 -top-40 -left-40 h-[600px] w-[800px] mesh-gradient pointer-events-none"
    >
    </div>
    <div
      class="absolute right-0 top-0 h-[500px] w-[500px] mesh-gradient pointer-events-none"
      style="opacity: 0.08;"
    >
    </div>

    <div class="relative z-10 max-w-3xl mx-auto text-center px-4">
      <span
        class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-full bg-link-bg-soft px-3 py-1 text-xs font-semibold text-link mb-6"
      >
        🔒 Secure Browser-Native Alternative
      </span>
      <h1
        class="text-4xl sm:text-5xl md:text-6xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink mb-6 font-sans"
      >
        A safer alternative to Chrome image downloader extensions.
      </h1>
      <p
        class="text-lg md:text-xl text-body mb-8 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl mx-auto"
      >
        Why risk your privacy installing extensions that read all your browsing data?
        Try our free, client-side web application that processes files entirely in your browser memory.
      </p>

      <div class="flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
        <a
          href="/url-list-downloader"
          class="rounded-pill bg-primary px-6 py-3 text-sm font-medium text-on-primary hover:bg-neutral-800 transition-colors shadow-sm"
        >
          Use URL List Downloader
        </a>
        <a
          href="/webpage-image-scraper"
          class="rounded-pill border border-hairline bg-canvas px-6 py-3 text-sm font-medium text-body hover:bg-canvas-soft-2 transition-colors"
        >
          Use Webpage Scraper
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Interactive Comparison Grid & Content Card -->
  <section class="max-w-[1000px] mx-auto mb-20">
    <div class="bg-canvas border border-hairline rounded-lg p-6 sm:p-8 md:p-12 shadow-sm">
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink mb-6 font-sans border-b border-hairline pb-4">
        Web App vs. Chrome Extensions: The Security Breakdown
      </h2>

      <div class="prose max-w-none text-sm text-body leading-relaxed space-y-6">
        <p>
          If you frequently download image assets, searching for a <strong>chrome bulk image downloader</strong> or a <strong>bulk image downloader chrome</strong> extension is a natural instinct. Chrome extensions are incredibly popular, but they come with hidden risks and operational constraints that many users overlook.
        </p>

        <p>
          Our web-based application is designed to offer all the features of a premium Chrome image extension—such as image filtering, resolution detection, visual grids, and ZIP packaging—without requiring you to install anything on your system.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink mt-8 mb-4 font-sans">
          The Hidden Security Risks of Browser Extensions
        </h3>
        
        <p>
          To understand why browser extensions are risky, you must look at their permission settings. Most batch image extensions require:
        </p>
        
        <blockquote class="border-l-4 border-link bg-canvas-soft-2 p-4 text-xs italic font-medium my-4">
          "Read and change all your data on the websites you visit"
        </blockquote>
        
        <p>
          This is a sweeping permission level. It allows the extension to inspect active page inputs, track your search history, read cookies, and monitor page transitions. While reputable developers might not abuse this, extensions are frequently sold to new owners who inject advertising code, telemetry scripts, or malicious hijackers.
        </p>

        <p>
          Because our <strong>free bulk image downloader</strong> runs inside a standard, sandboxed browser tab, it is isolated from the rest of your web history. It has absolutely no permission to scan your other tabs, read cookies from your bank accounts, or log your keystrokes.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink mt-8 mb-4 font-sans">
          Feature Comparison: Web App vs. Extensions
        </h3>

        <!-- Responsive Comparison Table -->
        <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6 border border-hairline rounded-sm">
          <table class="w-full text-left border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr class="bg-canvas-soft-2 text-ink text-xs font-mono border-b border-hairline">
                <th class="p-3 font-semibold">Feature / Attribute</th>
                <th class="p-3 font-semibold text-success">Our Client-Side Web App</th>
                <th class="p-3 font-semibold text-error">Typical Chrome Extension</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="text-xs">
              <tr class="border-b border-hairline">
                <td class="p-3 font-medium text-ink">Installation Required</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-success">❌ No installation</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-error">✅ Yes (Adds bloat to browser)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-hairline">
                <td class="p-3 font-medium text-ink">Security & Sandboxing</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-success">✅ Sandboxed in a standard tab</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-error">❌ High risk (Full read/write permissions)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-hairline">
                <td class="p-3 font-medium text-ink">Mobile Support</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-success">✅ Yes (iOS, Android Safari/Chrome)</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-error">❌ No (Chrome mobile doesn't support extensions)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-hairline">
                <td class="p-3 font-medium text-ink">ZIP Bundle Processing</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-success">✅ Done locally in browser cache</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-body">✅ Done locally in browser</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 font-medium text-ink">CORS Proxy Fallback</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-success">✅ Automatic server-side proxy fallback</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-body">⚠️ Varies (Requires custom rules)</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink mt-8 mb-4 font-sans">
          Platform Independence: Downloading on Mobile
        </h3>
        
        <p>
          One of the biggest limitations of a <strong>chrome image downloader extension</strong> is that mobile web browsers (like Chrome for Android or Safari for iPhone) do not support extensions. If you are on an iPad, a phone, or a Chromebook, extensions are completely unavailable.
        </p>
        
        <p>
          Because our tool is a responsive website built with Astro and Tailwind CSS, it adapts perfectly to mobile viewports. You can copy a list of image URLs on your smartphone, paste them into the downloader, package them into a ZIP file, and download the archive directly to your mobile file system.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-12 border-t border-hairline pt-8">
        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-6 font-sans">
          Frequently Asked Questions (Alternative Page)
        </h3>
        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="border-b border-hairline pb-4">
            <h4 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink mb-2">
              Why should I trust this online tool over a verified Chrome extension?
            </h4>
            <p class="text-sm text-body leading-relaxed">
              Google Chrome Store reviews extensions, but it cannot prevent verified extensions from changing owners and silently updating their code to collect data. Our tool is transparent: it runs in a standard website tab, has no access to your browser settings, and performs image downloading entirely on your local machine.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="border-b border-hairline pb-4">
            <h4 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink mb-2">
              Does this tool scrape sites that block web crawlers?
            </h4>
            <p class="text-sm text-body leading-relaxed">
              If a website blocks standard server-side fetching, our scraping API may not be able to read its HTML to locate images. In those cases, you can use our <strong>URL List Downloader</strong>: inspect the source code of the webpage yourself, copy the direct image URLs, and paste them directly into our downloader to bypass scraping blocks.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
